course: books
lesson: 60
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter IX, part 5: Wayfarers All (the spell, the Mole and the cure)
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: After lunch the Sea Rat leans close, his sea-grey eyes and the red glow of his glass holding the Water Rat «body and soul»; his talk seems to turn into sailors' songs, wind, gulls and waves, until the Rat can hear and see nothing else.
explain: Standing up, the Sea Rat tells of the little grey harbour town where he will wait for the right ship, pointing South, and then softly invites him: «And you, you will come too, young brother» — just bang the door behind you and step out.
explain: The voice fades, the stranger becomes a speck on the road, and the Rat, like a sleep-walker, packs a satchel, takes a stick and steps out of his door — just as the Mole arrives.
explain: The Mole sees that his friend's eyes have turned a streaked, shifting grey — «not his friend's eyes» — and drags him inside and holds him down; the Rat struggles, then shivers, sobs and falls asleep, while the Mole locks the satchel away.
explain: When the Rat wakes, his eyes are brown again but he is flat and sad; the tactful Mole chats about the harvest, apples and cosy winter, then leaves a pencil and paper by him, and soon the Rat is scribbling poetry: the cure has begun.
fact: The Seafarer «filled his glass with the red and glowing vintage of the South» (vintage = wine) and, «leaning towards the Water Rat, compelled his gaze and held him, body and soul».
fact: His eyes were «the changing foam-streaked grey-green of leaping Northern seas»; the glass shone «a hot ruby»: «The twin lights, the shifting grey and the steadfast red, mastered the Water Rat and held him bound, fascinated, powerless.»
fact: The talk seemed to pass into song: a «chanty of the sailors weighing the dripping anchor» (a chanty or shanty is a sailors' work song; to weigh anchor is to pull it up), and «chords of guitar and mandoline from gondola or caique» (a caique is a small Turkish boat).
fact: The Rat heard of «deep-sea fishings», «the tall bows of the great liner taking shape overhead through the fog», and «the merry home-coming» to «red-curtained windows».
fact: The Sea Rat will go to «the little grey sea town I know so well», with stone steps «overhung by great pink tufts of valerian» (a pink wild flower), and wait «till at last the right one lies waiting for me» — a ship with «her bowsprit pointing down harbour» (a bowsprit is the long pole at the front of a sailing ship).
fact: He pictures the start of the voyage: «the clink of the capstan» (a turning drum that winds in the anchor chain), «the jib and the foresail» (front sails), and at last «great green seas as she heels to the wind, pointing South!»
fact: «And you, you will come too, young brother; for the days pass, and never return, and the South still waits for you. Take the Adventure, heed the call, now ere the irrevocable moment passes!» (ere = before; irrevocable = that can never be taken back).
fact: «'Tis but a banging of the door behind you, a blithesome step forward, and you are out of the old life and into the new!» (blithesome = cheerful).
fact: «I will linger, and look back; and at last I will surely see you coming, eager and light-hearted, with all the South in your face!»
fact: The voice «died away and ceased as an insect's tiny trumpet dwindles swiftly into silence», and the Rat saw «but a distant speck on the white surface of the road».
fact: «Mechanically» (like a machine, without thinking) the Rat repacked the basket, went home, put «a few small necessaries and special treasures» in a satchel, chose «a stout stick for his wayfaring», and stepped out «just as the Mole appeared at the door».
fact: «Going South, with the rest of them,» he murmured, «Seawards first and then on shipboard, and so to the shores that are calling me!»
fact: The Mole saw his eyes «glazed and set and turned a streaked and shifting grey — not his friend's eyes, but the eyes of some other animal!»; he «dragged him inside, threw him down, and held him», then locked the satchel in a drawer.
fact: Later the Rat's eyes were «clear and dark and brown again as before», but he could not explain: «how could he put into cold words what had mostly been suggestion?»
fact: The Mole talked «casually» of the harvest, «the reddening apples», «the browning nuts», «jams and preserves and the distilling of cordials» (sweet fruit drinks), and then of midwinter, «and then he became simply lyrical» (spoke like a poem).
fact: The «tactful Mole» brought a pencil and paper: «It's quite a long time since you did any poetry»; later the Rat was «scribbling and sucking the top of his pencil», and «it was joy to the Mole to know that the cure had at least begun». Talk in this lesson: «bairn» (Scotland) = child; «mate» = friend; «lowkey» = a bit; «toque» (Canada) = woolly hat; «eh» (Canada) = right?
exercise: What does the Sea Rat fill his glass with? | Red wine from the South | Cold river water from the Rat's own jug | Hot sweet tea from a flask in his bundle | Ginger-beer from the Rat's luncheon-basket
exercise: What colour are the Sea Rat's eyes? | Grey-green, like Northern seas | Brown, clear and dark | Bright blue, like the sky | Red, like the wine in his glass
exercise: What are «the twin lights» that hold the Rat? | His grey eyes and the red glass | The sun and the moon in the sky | Two lanterns on a passing boat | The lights of Toad Hall at dusk
exercise: What does the Rat seem to hear as the talk goes on? | Sailors' songs, wind, gulls and waves | Only the cows cropping the grass behind | The Mole calling him home for supper | The far-off poop-poop of a motor-car
exercise: What is a «chanty»? | A sailors' work song | A small sailing boat for fishing | A heavy kind of anchor | A ship's cook in the galley
exercise: Where will the Sea Rat wait for his ship? | In a little grey sea town | In Venice by the Grand Canal | At the farm he has just left | In the Rat's hole by the river
exercise: What grows over the stone steps of that town? | Pink tufts of valerian | Dark ivy and prickly holly | Purple loosestrife | Red roses
exercise: What is a capstan? | A drum that winds in the anchor chain | A captain's hat with gold braid | A tin of ship's biscuits | A small flag flown at the top of the mast
exercise: «And you, you will come too, young brother.» What is the Sea Rat doing? | Inviting the Rat to leave and follow him | Telling the Rat to go home and stay there | Asking the Rat to lend him money for a ship | Saying goodbye for ever with no invitation
exercise: What does «irrevocable» mean? | That can never be taken back | That can be done again tomorrow | That is very loud | That nobody can see
exercise: What does the Sea Rat say leaving takes? | Just a banging of the door and a cheerful step | Many years of saving money and planning | A letter to every friend you have | Permission from Badger and the Mole and all his other friends on the river bank
exercise: How does the Sea Rat's voice end? | It dies away like an insect's tiny trumpet | It rises to a shout and wakes the whole river | It turns into the piping heard at the island | It stops suddenly when the Mole arrives
exercise: What does the Rat do after the stranger has gone? | Packs a satchel and sets off with a stick | Goes for a row on the river with the Mole | Writes a long poem about the sea at once | Runs after the stranger and calls him back
exercise: How does the Rat move about his room while packing? | Like a sleep-walker, slowly | In a wild hurry, throwing things | Singing and dancing for joy | Crying and stopping all the time
exercise: Who arrives at the door as the Rat steps out? | The Mole | Badger, from the wood | Toad, free at last | Otter, back from fishing
exercise: «Going South, with the rest of them.» Who are «the rest»? | The birds and travellers going south | The field-mice moving to their flats | The policemen chasing Toad | The weasels of the Wild Wood
exercise: What frightens the Mole about the Rat's eyes? | They are grey, not his friend's own eyes | They are closed, as if he is asleep | They are full of happy tears | They are red and sore from crying all afternoon
exercise: What does the Mole do? | Drags him inside and holds him down | Lets him go and waves goodbye | Runs to fetch Badger for help | Packs his own bag in a hurry and goes South with him
exercise: Where does the Mole put the satchel? | Locked in a drawer | Thrown in the river | Hidden away in the cold oven | Up on the roof of the house
exercise: What happens to the Rat after he stops struggling? | He shivers, sobs, then falls into a deep sleep | He jumps up and runs out of the back door | He laughs and says it was all a joke | He asks the Mole to come South with him and see the sea at last
exercise: How are the Rat's eyes when he wakes? | Clear, dark and brown again | Still grey and shifting | Green like the river | Closed and swollen from the long sleep
exercise: Why can the Rat not explain what happened? | It was mostly feeling, hard to put into cold words | He has promised the Sea Rat to keep it secret | He has forgotten it all, like the Piper's gift | He is far too angry with the Mole to speak a single word to him
exercise: What does the Mole talk about to cheer him? | The harvest, apples, nuts, jams and cosy winter | The South, the sea and the ports of Italy | Toad's adventures and the castle dungeon | The Wild Wood at night and the weasels' wicked little faces
exercise: What does the Mole leave by his friend's elbow? | A pencil and paper | A big map of the South | A hot cup of cocoa and toast | His old satchel, unpacked
exercise: What is the Rat doing when the Mole peeps in later? | Scribbling and sucking his pencil | Packing his satchel again | Staring out at the road | Fast asleep in his chair with his mouth open
exercise: Looking back: in chapter II, after the motor-car passed, what did the Rat say about Toad's trance? | «He is now possessed» | «He is only very tired» | «He is very clever indeed» | «He will soon be well»
exercise: Looking back: in chapter VI, what did the friends do to stop Toad going after motor-cars? | Locked him in his bedroom and guarded him | Sent him on a long sea voyage | Sold all his money to Badger | Took him away to live quietly at Mole End for the summer
exercise: Looking back: in chapter V, who quietly cheered whom up at Mole End, without fuss? | The Rat cheered up the Mole | The Mole cheered up the Rat | Badger cheered up Toad | The field-mice cheered up Badger
exercise: ★★★ «The shifting grey and the steadfast red». Which is which? | Grey = the Sea Rat's eyes; red = the wine | Grey = the road; red = the Rat's eyes | Grey = the river; red = the sunset | Grey = the Mole's coat; red = the satchel
test: «Ratty lowkey wanted to go, so the Mole should've let him.» (lowkey = a bit) What does the book show? | He was under a spell, «not his friend's eyes»; the Mole saved him | He had planned it for years, and the Mole was unfair | He wanted to go, and the Mole went with him | He only wanted a walk to the village to buy new pencils and paper
test: Which is TRUE about this part? | The Rat's eyes turn grey like the Sea Rat's, then brown again | The Rat sails away on a ship that very evening | The Mole decides to go South instead | The Sea Rat comes back along the road to fetch the Rat
test: Isla from Aberdeen: «the wee bairn of a Mole just let his pal walk off.» (bairn = child) Put it right. | He grappled with him and dragged him inside | He waved and wished him luck on the road | He ran away and hid in the cellar | He wasn't there at all that evening, being out with Badger
test: Why does the Mole talk «casually» about the harvest instead of telling the Rat off? | Gently turning his mind back to home and its seasons works better | He hasn't noticed anything is wrong with the Rat | He wants the Rat to help bring in the harvest | He is bored with the Rat and just wants to talk about food
test: Why does the Mole suggest poetry? | It gives the Rat something of his own to do, instead of brooding | He wants the Rat to write a poem about the Sea Rat | He needs a poem for the field-mice's play | He thinks poetry will make the Rat sleepy so he can go to bed early
test: Jack from Vancouver, in his toque (woolly hat): «the Rat's cured completely by the end, eh?» (eh = right?) Put it right. | No: «the cure had at least begun» — just begun | Yes, completely, and he never thinks of the sea again | No, he leaves for the South that night | Yes, and he becomes a sailor next spring
test: What would probably have happened if the Mole had arrived five minutes later? | The Rat might have been far down the road, following the Sea Rat | The Rat would have unpacked by himself and gone to bed | The Sea Rat would have come back to stop him | Nothing at all; the Rat was only going down to the river for a row
test: «The Sea Rat is a bad guy, mate, a villain.» (mate = friend) What is fairer? | Not a villain: he follows his own call, but his spell is dangerous for the Rat | Yes: he is a thief who steals the Rat's basket | Yes: he is a weasel in disguise | No: he is really the Piper from chapter VII, come back in disguise to test the Rat
test: «'Tis but a banging of the door behind you.» What is tricky about that line? | It makes a huge change sound tiny and easy | It is a real instruction for fixing doors | It is a warning to shut the door against the cold | It means the Rat's door is broken
test: Looking back: in chapter I, the Rat said the Wide World «doesn't matter, either to you or me». What nearly happened today? | He nearly set off into the Wide World himself | He moved to the Wild Wood | He bought a motor-car | He joined the field-mice in their new flats near the Stores
test: Looking back: in chapter VII, the Rat was «rapt, transported» by the Piper's music. How is the Sea Rat's spell different? | It pulls him away from home and friends; the Piper's call led him to help | It is exactly the same and leads to Portly | The Sea Rat's spell makes him sleep for a year | The Piper's music made him want to go South too, with the swallows
test: Looking back: in chapter VI, Toad was held down by his friends «for his own good». Who is held down for his own good today? | The Rat, by the Mole | Toad, by the gaoler | The Mole, by Badger | The Sea Rat, by the Rat
test: Looking back: in chapter I, the Mole knew nothing of boats or rivers. How has he changed by now? | He is wise and calm enough to rescue and comfort his teacher | He still knows nothing and needs the Rat for everything | He has become a sailor himself | He has gone back to live at Mole End for good, alone
test: Looking back: in chapter III, the Rat sat by the fire «trying over rhymes that wouldn't fit». What does the Mole use that love of his now for? | To help him heal, with a pencil and paper | To win a poetry prize | To make him write a letter to Toad | To keep him busy so he forgets all about lunch and supper
test: ★★★ Careful: when the Rat's eyes are grey, whose eyes do they look like? | The Sea Rat's grey-green eyes | The Mole's little eyes | The Piper's kindly eyes | Toad's motoring goggles
test: ★★★ The Mole's talk goes: harvest, then apples and nuts, then jams and cordials, then midwinter. Why that order? | It walks step by step from now towards cosy winter at home | It is the order of the Sea Rat's ports | It goes from winter back to spring | It is in alphabetical order, as in the dictionary
exam: Identify the agencies described as having «mastered the Water Rat and held him bound». | The shifting grey of the seafarer's eyes and the steadfast red of the wine | The roar of the weir and the piping of the Friend and Helper | The voices of the three swallows and the field-mice | The rattle of the anchor-chain and the hungry cry of the gulls and sea-mews
exam: State the condition of the Water Rat's eyes as observed by the Mole upon his attempted departure. | Glazed and set, and turned a streaked and shifting grey | Clear, dark and brown, as was customary | Closed in sleep, he walking as a somnambulist | Red and swollen from protracted weeping occasioned by grief
exam: Ascertain the course adopted by the Mole in respect of his companion's satchel. | He threw it into a drawer and locked it | He returned it to the seafarer upon the road | He unpacked its contents upon the parlour table | He conveyed it to the Badger for safe keeping
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the conclusion of the ninth chapter? | The Water Rat, supplied with pencil and paper, began to compose verse, and the cure was said to have begun | The Water Rat departed for the South at nightfall, the Mole accompanying him | The Water Rat remained wholly silent and refused all occupation | The seafarer returned and was persuaded to remain upon the river bank as a lodger with the Water Rat
